mode: "100644"
text: "# Audio & Camera — the superpower\n\nVeo's defining advantage is **native synchronized audio**, and its cinematic power comes from\n**camera/motion direction.** Prompt both deliberately.\n\n> Re-verify quarterly. Audio and motion are strong but **experimental and improving** — always review\n> the output.\n\n## Native audio (the differentiator)\n\nVeo generates **video and audio in a single pass** — dialogue with lip-sync, sound effects, ambient\nsound, and music — temporally matched to the action. This is what sets it apart. How to prompt it:\n\n- **Dialogue:** put the **exact line in quotes**, and say who speaks and how. e.g. *…the chef looks to\n  camera and says, \"Here's the only step people skip,\" in a calm, confident tone.* Keep lines short for\n  reliable lip-sync.\n- **Sound effects:** name them and when (\"the pan sizzles as the steak hits\"; \"a soft click\").\n- **Ambient:** set the soundscape (\"quiet café murmur, distant traffic\").\n- **Music:** describe mood/style if you want underscore (\"soft jazz in the background\").\n- **An explicit audio cue in the prompt meaningfully improves** the native audio — don't leave it to\n  chance; describe what should be heard.\n\n**Honest handling of audio:**\n- Treat generated audio as a **draft / guide track** for branded work — for the final, many teams\n  **record real voiceover, license music, and mix** to spec, using Veo's audio as timing reference.\n- **Verify** the audio (wrong words, off lip-sync, odd artifacts happen). A generation can be **blocked\n  by audio safety filters** (you aren't charged when blocked) — rephrase if so.\n- Generated **voices/likenesses** must not imitate real, identifiable people (see safety in the SKILL).\n\n## Camera & motion (the cinematic layer)\n\nDirect the camera like a filmmaker — this is most of the \"cinematic\" feel:\n\n- **Shot type:** wide / establishing / medium / close-up / macro / POV / over-the-shoulder.\n- **Movement:** static / slow push-in (dolly) / pull-back / pan / tilt / tracking / crane / 180° arc /\n  handheld.\n- **Angle & lens:** low/high angle, eye-level; \"shallow depth of field,\" \"wide lens,\" \"lens flare.\"\n- **Motion of the subject:** describe it physically (\"steam rising,\" \"fabric billowing in the wind\") —\n  Veo's physics handle realistic movement when you describe it concretely.\n- **One clear motion per clip.** In ~8 seconds, a single, well-described camera/subject move reads far\n  better than three competing ones.\n\n## The honest caveats\n\n- **Artifacts happen** — small faces, hands, fine textures, complex physics can glitch; **review every\n  clip** before publishing.\n- **Vertical framing** — 9:16 is native, but wide/group scenes can feel cramped; **explicitly describe\n  vertical framing** (\"subject centered, vertical composition\") for mobile.\n- **Consistency across clips** can drift — use reference images/first-last frame\n  (`inputs-length-and-recipes.md`) and review the set.\n"
